#!/bin/sh
# Reject a commit message with no `LWB-Agent:` trailer, and reject a
# commit whose author identity is not LEAPWare or an allow-listed bot.
#
# scripts/lwb_lanes.py already enforces the trailer in CI, but only after
# the commit exists and has been pushed -- and the only repair at that
# point is a history rewrite, which this machine's permission settings
# deny outright. That cost a branch rebuild on PR #14.
#
# The identity check added here exists for the SAME reason, proven again
# and again on 2026-09-24: scripts/lwb_check_commit_identity.py enforces
# `ALLOWED_HUMAN = ("LEAPWare", "leapware@outlook.com")` in CI, and a
# commit authored under any other name/email fails that check permanently
# once pushed -- force-push denied, same as the trailer case. This defect
# class caused several unrecoverable re-cuts in one day on PR #55 alone
# (and its predecessors #54, #53, #52, #50, #39), from at least three
# different concurrently-running sessions that each got git identity wrong
# in a different way -- a stray `git config --global user.name`, an
# account-login-shaped name instead of the exact required one, the
# generic `Claude <noreply@anthropic.com>` default. Every one of those was
# free to fix at commit time and cost a whole branch re-cut once pushed.
# This hook moves the check to the moment the commit is written, mirroring
# the trailer check already living here, for the identical reason: fixing
# it here is free, fixing it after a push is not.
#
# Put in commit-msg rather than pre-commit because the author identity is
# already fully resolved by the time commit-msg runs (`git var
# GIT_AUTHOR_IDENT` reads it) even though the commit object does not exist
# yet -- so a bad identity is still caught before anything is written, in
# the same hook and the same run that already inspects the message, rather
# than adding a second hook file to maintain in parallel with this one.
#
# Lives under scripts/ rather than .githooks/ on purpose: scripts/ is a
# shared path, and a repo-wide gate belongs to no single CLI's lane.
#
# Not installed by cloning. Enable it with:
#   git config core.hooksPath scripts/githooks
# See docs/maintainers/session-protocol.md.
#
# `human` is deliberately NOT accepted here: lwb_lanes.py reads this
# trailer to decide which lane a commit belongs to, and CI rejects a
# `human` trailer on an agent-authored commit.
#
# The identity allowlist below is a hand-mirror of
# scripts/lwb_check_commit_identity.py's ALLOWED_HUMAN, BOT_NAME and
# BOT_EMAIL -- kept in sync by hand since this is a POSIX shell hook and
# that script is Python; if that script's allowlist ever changes, this
# one must change with it, or the hook diverges from the gate it exists to
# preempt.

msg_file="$1"

if ! grep -qE '^LWB-Agent: (claude|codex)$' "$msg_file"; then
	cat >&2 <<'EOF'
commit-msg: missing `LWB-Agent:` trailer.

Every commit needs a trailer line, exactly:

    LWB-Agent: claude

(or `codex`). scripts/lwb_lanes.py reads it to decide which lane the
commit belongs to. A commit without one fails the lwb-lanes CI job, and by
then the only repair is a history rewrite.

Add the trailer and commit again.
EOF
	exit 1
fi

ident=$(git var GIT_AUTHOR_IDENT) || exit 1
author_name=$(printf '%s\n' "$ident" | sed -n 's/^\(.*\) <[^>]*>.*/\1/p')
author_email=$(printf '%s\n' "$ident" | sed -n 's/.*<\([^>]*\)>.*/\1/p')

allowed_human_name="LEAPWare"
allowed_human_email="leapware@outlook.com"

is_allowed=0
if [ "$author_name" = "$allowed_human_name" ] && [ "$author_email" = "$allowed_human_email" ]; then
	is_allowed=1
elif printf '%s\n' "$author_name" | grep -qE '^[[:alnum:]_.-]+\[bot\]$' \
	&& printf '%s\n' "$author_email" | grep -qE '^([0-9]+\+)?[[:alnum:]_.-]+\[bot\]@users\.noreply\.github\.com$'; then
	is_allowed=1
fi

if [ "$is_allowed" -ne 1 ]; then
	cat >&2 <<EOF
commit-msg: commit author is not an allowed identity.

    author: ${author_name} <${author_email}>
    required: ${allowed_human_name} <${allowed_human_email}>
    (or a GitHub-managed bot, e.g. dependabot[bot] <NNNN+dependabot[bot]@users.noreply.github.com>)

scripts/lwb_check_commit_identity.py enforces this exact allowlist in CI,
AFTER the commit is pushed -- and by then the only repair is a history
rewrite, which this machine's permission settings deny outright. This
exact defect has cost several unrecoverable branch re-cuts in one day
(PR #55 and its predecessors). Fix it now, before it is unrecoverable:

    git config user.name "${allowed_human_name}"
    git config user.email "${allowed_human_email}"

(repo-local, no --global -- check \`git config user.name\` /
\`git config user.email\` actually report what you expect before
committing again; an ambient global config can silently override what you
think is set). Then commit again.
EOF
	exit 1
fi

exit 0
